WASHINGTON, D.C. – Congresswoman Loretta Sanchez (CA-46), founder and co-chair of the Women in the Military Caucus and senior member of the House Armed Services Committee, today released the following statement in response to the Pentagon’s release of executive actions to curb sexual assaults in the military.
“I am heartened to see the Pentagon take such substantive steps to prevent sexual assaults and better support victims. The Pentagon is demonstrating that they hear Congress loud and clear: sexual assaults by and among members of our military forces are deplorable, inexcusable and embarrassingly widespread.
“These actions are a starting point, but more can and should be done. The House FY14 National Defense Authorization Act includes language I introduced that creates a more effective and accurate performance evaluation process throughout the chain of command. I believe and am optimistic that the solutions I put forward will be included in a final bill.
“I want to emphasize that taking responsibility for investigation or prosecution of sexual assault away from the chain of command, as some recent legislative proposals would do, would undermine commanders and transfer responsibility for these crucial military standards to a remote, civilian bureaucracy.  
“I am glad the issue is being taken seriously by our armed forces. It is time to address the unacceptable number of sexual assaults by holding commanders accountable, stopping abuse before it starts, tracking inappropriate behavior to prevent it and changing the culture from the inside out.”
 
The Pentagon’s measures today include:
 Creating a legal advocacy program in each military service that will provide legal representation to sexual assault victims throughout the judicial process;
Ensuring that pretrial investigative hearings of sexual assault-related charges are conducted by JAG officers;
Providing commanders with options to reassign or transfer a member who is accused of committing a sexual assault or related offense in order to eliminate continued contact while respecting the rights of both victims and the accused;
Requiring timely follow-up reports on sexual assault incidents and responses to be given to the first general or flag officer within the chain of command;
Directing DoD's Inspector General to regularly evaluate closed sexual assault investigations;
Standardizing prohibitions on inappropriate behavior between recruiters and trainers and their recruits and trainees across the Department, and;
Developing and proposing changes to the Manual for Courts-Martial that would allow victims to give input during the sentencing phase of courts-martial.
